@startuml

title How the engine knows a service is gone

start

:engine publishes a step command to a subject;

note right
  The bus drops a command whose subject has
  no subscriber, and says nothing about it.
  From the engine's side a dead service and a
  slow one are the same silence, which is why
  the deadline decides and presence only
  explains.
end note

:engine waits, and each pass asks\n"has this dispatch been silent\nlonger than its budget?";

if (past the budget?) then (no)
    :keep waiting;
    stop
else (yes)
    :fail the step, naming the subject\nand how long it has been silent;
    :read what the engine knows about\nthe component that owns that subject;
    note right
      Every service publishes a heartbeat, and the
      engine keeps the last time it heard from each.
      The component is the subject's first token:
      marketdata.v1.… belongs to ores.marketdata.service.
    end note
    if (heard from it at all?) then (no)
        :"Nothing has reported as the marketdata\nservice since this engine started, so the\ncommand may have had no handler.";
    else (yes)
        if (reporting when the command\nwas published?) then (no)
            :"The marketdata service was not reporting\nwhen the command was published: it last\nreported 11m 3s before it.";
        else (yes)
            :"The marketdata service was still reporting\nwhen the command was published, so the command\nreached a service that did not finish the step.";
        endif
    endif
    :stop the run with that reason, so a person\nreads a cause and not a step that looks busy;
    stop
endif

@enduml
